/K [6]
>>
endobj
1328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (These findings were not merely theoretical. The practical exploitation phase demonstrated that an attacker with local access to a domain-joined workstation could obtain Domain Administrator privileges without cracking a single password. This practical validation significantly increased the severity of the identified risks.)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (Critical Findings)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (The assessment identified five critical security findings, which ultimately converged into three primary attack vectors capable of leading to complete Active Directory compromise.)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (1. Print Spooler Vulnerability)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (The Print Spooler service was enabled on the Domain Controller.)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (Although commonly overlooked, this configuration represents a well-known and highly critical security weakness. It allows an attacker to coerce the Domain Controller into authenticating and subsequently perform a DCSync attack to replicate Active Directory secrets.)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (During the assessment, this attack chain demonstrated that privileged account credentials could be obtained within approximately two minutes, resulting in complete administrative control of the domain.)
/Pg 105 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (2. Kerberos Delegation Misconfiguration)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (Privileged administrative accounts were not configured as "Account is sensitive and cannot be delegated.")
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (As a result, when a privileged administrator authenticated to a compromised system, Kerberos authentication tickets remained available in memory and could be extracted by an attacker.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (The assessment successfully demonstrated this scenario by exporting and injecting a legitimate Kerberos ticket, allowing the complete impersonation of a Domain Administrator without knowledge of the user's password.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (3. Legacy NTLM Authentication Still Enabled)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (Legacy NTLM and LAN Manager \(LM\) authentication protocols remained enabled throughout the domain.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (This configuration allowed the successful extraction of an administrator's NTLM hash from LSASS memory, followed by a Pass-the-Hash attack that provided full administrative access in approximately 30 seconds.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (Because Pass-the-Hash attacks replay authentication hashes directly rather than recovering plaintext passwords, password complexity alone cannot mitigate this threat while NTLM remains available.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (The most significant observation is that these three attack paths coexisted simultaneously, providing multiple independent routes to complete domain compromise.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (Consequently, mitigating only one attack vector would not have been sufficient. Even if one path were eliminated, the remaining vectors would still allow an attacker to obtain administrative control of the Active Directory environment.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (This assessment therefore highlighted the need for a defense-in-depth approach, combining service hardening, privileged account protection, modern authentication protocols, and credential protection mechanisms to effectively eliminate the demonstrated attack chains.)
/Pg 106 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1346 0 obj
<<
