4061 0 obj
<<
/Title (Positive finding. No computer or user accounts with Service Principal Names \(SPNs\) were configured for unconstrained Kerberos delegation. This result should be interpreted alongside the critical Print Spooler finding previously identified by PingCastle and Purple Knight \(Figures 34 and 42\). PingCastle explains that exploiting the Print Spooler vulnerability to perform a DCSync attack generally requires the presence of an account configured with unconstrained delegation. Purple Knight's result indicates that no such accounts were detected, providing an important nuance for the report: Although the Print Spooler service remains a security concern, its exploitation currently represents a latent risk rather than an immediately exploitable attack path. Should an unconstrained delegation configuration be introduced in the future while the Print Spooler service remains enabled, exploitation would become immediately possible.)
/Dest [60 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4063 0 R
/Next 4059 0 R
>>
endobj
4062 0 obj
<<
/Title (sali-adm \(salma.ouchahed\) karim-adm \(karim.jamali\) This finding compounds the previous weakness, as weak passwords that never expire significantly increase the exposure window following account compromise.)
/Dest [47 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4060 0 R
/Next 4064 0 R
>>
endobj
4063 0 obj
<<
/Title (No accounts were found with suspicious mappings configured through the altSecurityIdentities attribute. This attribute is used to map X.509 certificates or external Kerberos identities to Active Directory accounts. If an attacker successfully modifies this attribute, they can impersonate the targeted account, making it a stealthy persistence technique that is rarely used in standard enterprise deployments. No exposure was identified during the assessment.)
/Dest [59 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4065 0 R
/Next 4061 0 R
>>
endobj
